    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="hwe">
      <h2>1. Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium (HWE) & Statistical Testing</h2>
      <p>
        In an infinitely large, panmictic (random-mating) diploid population with non-overlapping generations, and in the absence of mutation, migration, and natural selection, allele and genotype frequencies remain constant from generation to generation.
      </p>

      <h3>Mathematical Derivation</h3>
      <p>
        Consider a single biallelic locus with alleles <Katex tex="A" /> and <Katex tex="a" /> having population frequencies <Katex tex="p = P(A)" /> and <Katex tex="q = P(a) = 1 - p" />.
        Under random union of gametes, the probability of obtaining each diploid genotype is the product of their independent gametic probabilities:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Hardy-Weinberg Genotype Proportions</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="P(AA) = p^2, \quad P(Aa) = 2pq, \quad P(aa) = q^2 \implies p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = (p + q)^2 = 1" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          Degrees of Freedom: <Katex tex="\text{df} = 3 - 1 - 1 = 1" /> (3 genotype classes minus 1 for fixed total sample size minus 1 for estimated parameter <Katex tex="p" />).
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Testing Departures from HWE</h3>
      <p>
        In empirical genomic datasets and GWAS quality control, departures from HWE are evaluated using Pearson's <Katex tex="\chi^2" /> goodness-of-fit test or Wigginton's exact test:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Pearson's Chi-Square Test for HWE</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\chi^2 = \frac{(N_{AA} - N p^2)^2}{N p^2} + \frac{(N_{Aa} - 2Npq)^2}{2Npq} + \frac{(N_{aa} - N q^2)^2}{N q^2} \sim \chi^2_1" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-warning">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={15} /> Biological vs. Technical HWE Violations
        </div>
        <p>
          In disease association studies, extreme departures from HWE in control samples (<Katex tex="P < 10^{-6}" />) almost universally indicate <strong>genotyping artifacts</strong> (e.g. allele dropout, probe hybridization bias, non-specific binding, or batch effects).
          <br/>
          In contrast, mild HWE departures in case samples can represent genuine biological signals of recessive or dominant disease risk mechanisms (Wigginton et al. 2005).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="drift">
      <h2>2. Wright-Fisher Model & Stochastic Genetic Drift</h2>
      <p>
        In finite populations of size <Katex tex="N" /> (containing <Katex tex="2N" /> gametes in diploids), random binomial sampling of gametes across generations causes allele frequencies to fluctuate stochastically—a fundamental evolutionary force termed <strong>genetic drift</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3>Transition Probability Matrix</h3>
      <p>
        Under the Wright-Fisher model with non-overlapping generations, if an allele has <Katex tex="j" /> copies in generation <Katex tex="t" /> (frequency <Katex tex="p = j / 2N" />), the probability that it has <Katex tex="k" /> copies in generation <Katex tex="t+1" /> follows a binomial distribution:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Wright-Fisher Binomial Transition Probability</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="P_{jk} = P(X_{t+1} = k \mid X_t = j) = \binom{2N}{k} \left(\frac{j}{2N}\right)^k \left(1 - \frac{j}{2N}\right)^{2N - k}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="X_t \in \{0, 1, \dots, 2N\}" /> is the discrete Markov chain state representing the allele copy count.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Decay of Expected Heterozygosity</h3>
      <p>
        Genetic drift inexorably erodes genetic variation. The expected heterozygosity <Katex tex="H_t = 2p_t(1 - p_t)" /> decreases by a factor of <Katex tex="1 / (2N_e)" /> per generation:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Generational Heterozygosity Decay</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="H_t = H_0 \left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right)^t \approx H_0 \exp\left(-\frac{t}{2N_e}\right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="N_e" /> is the <strong>effective population size</strong>, reflecting the size of an idealized panmictic population experiencing the same rate of genetic drift as the actual population.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Fixation Probability and Time to Fixation</h3>
      <p>
        For a neutral allele with initial frequency <Katex tex="p_0" />:
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Probability of Ultimate Fixation:</strong> <Katex tex="P(\text{fixation}) = p_0" />. For a new single neutral mutation (<Katex tex="p_0 = 1 / 2N" />), <Katex tex="P(\text{fix}) = \frac{1}{2N}" />.
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Expected Time to Fixation (Conditional on Fixation):</strong> <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[T_{\text{fix}} \mid \text{fixed}] \approx 4N_e" /> generations (Kimura & Ohta 1969).
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Expected Time to Loss:</strong> <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[T_{\text{loss}}] \approx 2 \ln(2N)" /> generations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="coalescent">
      <h2>3. Kingman's Coalescent Theory & TMRCA Derivation</h2>
      <p>
        While the Wright-Fisher model tracks populations forward in time, <strong>Kingman's Coalescent (1982)</strong> models the genealogy of <Katex tex="n" /> sampled lineages looking <em>backward</em> in time.
      </p>

      <h3>Coalescent Rate & Exponential Waiting Times</h3>
      <p>
        When tracing <Katex tex="k" /> distinct lineages backward, each generation there are <Katex tex="\binom{k}{2}" /> possible pairs of lineages. In a diploid population of effective size <Katex tex="N_e" />, the probability that a specific pair shares a common parent in the previous generation is <Katex tex="1 / (2N_e)" />.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the continuous-time limit (<Katex tex="N_e \to \infty" />, rescaling time in units of <Katex tex="2N_e" /> generations), the waiting time <Katex tex="T_k" /> during which there are exactly <Katex tex="k" /> ancestral lineages is exponentially distributed:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Coalescent Rate & Expected Waiting Interval</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="T_k \sim \text{Exp}\left(\lambda_k\right), \quad \lambda_k = \frac{\binom{k}{2}}{2N_e} = \frac{k(k-1)}{4N_e}, \qquad \mathbb{E}[T_k] = \frac{4N_e}{k(k-1)}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Expected Time to Most Recent Common Ancestor (TMRCA)</h3>
      <p>
        Summing the expected waiting times across all coalescent events from <Katex tex="k = n" /> down to <Katex tex="k = 2" /> gives the expected height of the ancestral genealogy:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Expected TMRCA & Total Genealogical Tree Length</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[T_{\text{MRCA}}] = \sum_{k=2}^{n} \mathbb{E}[T_k] = \sum_{k=2}^{n} \frac{4N_e}{k(k-1)} = 4N_e \left(1 - \frac{1}{n}\right) \xrightarrow{n \to \infty} 4N_e \text{ generations}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <strong>Total Tree Length:</strong> <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[L_{\text{tot}}] = \sum_{k=2}^n k \mathbb{E}[T_k] = 4N_e \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \frac{1}{i}" />, which scales with the harmonic series <Katex tex="a_1 = \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \frac{1}{i}" />.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="tajima">
      <h2>4. Tajima's D Statistic & Tests of Neutrality</h2>
      <p>
        Under neutral mutation-drift equilibrium with mutation rate <Katex tex="\mu" /> per locus per generation, the fundamental population genetic parameter is <Katex tex="\theta = 4N_e \mu" />.
      </p>

      <h3>Two Competing Estimators of θ</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>1. Average Pairwise Nucleotide Diversity (<Katex tex="\hat{\theta}_\pi" />):</strong>
        <br/>
        Measures the average number of pairwise nucleotide differences across all sample pairs:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\theta}_\pi = \pi = \frac{1}{\binom{n}{2}} \sum_{i < j} k_{ij} = \sum_{l=1}^S \frac{2 n_l (n - n_l)}{n(n-1)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\pi" /> is heavily weighted toward intermediate-frequency alleles.
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>2. Watterson's Estimator from Segregating Sites (<Katex tex="\hat{\theta}_S" />):</strong>
        <br/>
        Derived from the total number of polymorphic (segregating) sites <Katex tex="S" />:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\theta}_S = \frac{S}{a_1}, \quad a_1 = \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \frac{1}{i}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\theta}_S" /> weights all polymorphic sites equally, making it highly sensitive to rare singletons.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Tajima's D Formulation & Biological Interpretation</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Tajima's D Statistic (Tajima 1989)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D = \frac{\hat{\theta}_\pi - \hat{\theta}_S}{\sqrt{\widehat{\operatorname{Var}}(\hat{\theta}_\pi - \hat{\theta}_S)}}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Value of D</th>
              <th>Allele Frequency Spectrum</th>
              <th>Biological & Evolutionary Mechanisms</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="D \approx 0" /></strong></td>
              <td>Neutral mutation-drift equilibrium</td>
              <td>Standard neutral evolution; Kingman coalescent expectation holds.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="D < 0" /></strong></td>
              <td>Excess of rare alleles / singletons (<Katex tex="\hat{\theta}_\pi < \hat{\theta}_S" />)</td>
              <td><strong>Positive directional selection (selective sweep)</strong>, recovery from a recent selective sweep, or <strong>rapid demographic population expansion</strong>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="D > 0" /></strong></td>
              <td>Excess of intermediate-frequency alleles (<Katex tex="\hat{\theta}_\pi > \hat{\theta}_S" />)</td>
              <td><strong>Balancing selection (heterozygote advantage)</strong>, frequency-dependent selection, or <strong>sudden demographic population bottleneck / population subdivision</strong>.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="fisher1918">
      <h2>5. The Fisher 1918 Synthesis & Infinitesimal Model</h2>
      <p>
        In 1918, <strong>Ronald Aylmer Fisher</strong> published his foundational paper, <em>"The Correlation Between Relatives on the Supposition of Mendelian Inheritance"</em>, resolving the fierce debate between Mendelians (particulate inheritance) and Biometricians (continuous Gaussian variation).
      </p>

      <h3>The Infinitesimal Convergence Theorem</h3>
      <p>
        Fisher proved that if a trait is determined by <Katex tex="M" /> independent Mendelian loci where each locus has an additive allele substitution effect <Katex tex="\beta_j" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Additive Infinitesimal Model</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="y_i = \mu + \sum_{j=1}^{M} \beta_j X_{ij} + \epsilon_i, \quad \beta_j \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, \frac{\sigma_g^2}{M}\right), \quad \epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_e^2)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          As <Katex tex="M \to \infty" />, by the <strong>Lindeberg-Feller Central Limit Theorem</strong>, the polygenic genetic value <Katex tex="g_i = \sum \beta_j X_{ij}" /> converges rigorously to a Gaussian distribution <Katex tex="g_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_g^2)" />, producing a continuous bell-shaped phenotypic distribution <Katex tex="y_i \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \sigma_g^2 + \sigma_e^2)" />.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Covariance Between Biological Relatives</h3>
      <p>
        Fisher derived exact formulas for phenotypic covariance between relatives based on shared alleles identical by descent (IBD):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Covariance Between Relatives under HWE</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\operatorname{Cov}(y_1, y_2) = 2 \Phi_{12} V_A + \Delta_{12} V_D" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\Phi_{12}" /> = Kinship coefficient (probability that two alleles sampled at random are IBD) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\Delta_{12}" /> = Probability that individuals share both alleles IBD.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Relationship Pair</th>
              <th>Kinship (<Katex tex="\Phi_{12}" />)</th>
              <th>Dual IBD (<Katex tex="\Delta_{12}" />)</th>
              <th>Expected Phenotypic Covariance</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Monozygotic (MZ) Twins</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="1/2" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="1" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="V_A + V_D + V_C" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Parent-Offspring</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="1/4" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="0" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\frac{1}{2}V_A" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Full Siblings</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="1/4" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="1/4" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\frac{1}{2}V_A + \frac{1}{4}V_D + V_C" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Half Siblings</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="1/8" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="0" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\frac{1}{4}V_A" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>First Cousins</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="1/16" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="0" /></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\frac{1}{8}V_A" /></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="variance">
      <h2>6. Master Phenotypic Variance Partitioning</h2>
      <p>
        Starting from the fundamental definition of phenotype as a sum of genetic and environmental influences (<Katex tex="P = G + E" />):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Complete Phenotypic Variance Decomposition</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="V_P = V_G + V_E + 2\operatorname{Cov}(G, E) = V_A + V_D + V_I + V_{G \times E} + 2\operatorname{Cov}(G, E) + V_E" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <strong>P0 Orthogonality Rule:</strong> The terms <Katex tex="V_A, V_D, V_I" /> are mutually orthogonal only under random mating (HWE) with no Linkage Disequilibrium. In the presence of assortative mating or LD, genetic variance components have non-zero covariances.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Variance Component Definitions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><Katex tex="V_A" /> (Additive Genetic Variance):</strong> Variance of breeding values (the linear regression of genotype on phenotype). This is the only component that responds predictably to directional selection.</li>
        <li><strong><Katex tex="V_D" /> (Dominance Variance):</strong> Non-linear interactions between homologous alleles at the same locus (intra-locus dominance).</li>
        <li><strong><Katex tex="V_I = V_{AA} + V_{AD} + V_{DD}" /> (Epistatic Variance):</strong> Non-linear interaction variance between distinct genetic loci across the genome.</li>
        <li><strong><Katex tex="V_{G \times E}" /> (Gene-by-Environment Interaction):</strong> Variance arising when the phenotypic effect of a genotype varies across distinct environmental regimes.</li>
        <li><strong><Katex tex="\operatorname{Cov}(G, E)" /> (Genotype-Environment Covariance):</strong> Non-random distribution of genotypes across environments (e.g. passive genetic nurture, active niche-seeking).</li>
        <li><strong><Katex tex="V_E" /> (Environmental Noise):</strong> Residual micro-environmental fluctuations, developmental noise, and measurement error.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 7 -->
    <section id="heritability">
      <h2>7. Narrow-Sense vs. Broad-Sense Heritability & The Breeder's Equation</h2>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Broad-Sense vs. Narrow-Sense Heritability</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="H^2 = \frac{V_G}{V_P} = \frac{V_A + V_D + V_I}{V_P} \quad (\text{Broad-Sense}), \qquad h^2 = \frac{V_A}{V_P} \quad (\text{Narrow-Sense})" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The Breeder's Equation</h3>
      <p>
        Narrow-sense heritability <Katex tex="h^2" /> quantifies the proportion of phenotypic variance transmitted faithfully from parents to offspring. The evolutionary response to selection is governed by <strong>Lush's Breeder's Equation (1937)</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">The Univariate Breeder's Equation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="R = h^2 S \iff (\mu' - \mu) = \frac{V_A}{V_P} (\mu_s - \mu)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="R = \mu' - \mu" /> = Response to selection (difference between offspring generation mean and parental generation mean) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="S = \mu_s - \mu" /> = Selection differential (difference between selected breeder mean and parental population mean).
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Lande's Multivariate Selection Equation (1979)</h3>
      <p>
        For multiple genetically correlated traits, selection on one trait induces correlated evolutionary responses across all traits:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Multivariate Breeder's Equation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\Delta \bar{\mathbf{z}} = \mathbf{G} \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{s} = \mathbf{G} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{\text{grad}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{G}" /> = Additive genetic variance-covariance matrix &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{P}" /> = Phenotypic variance-covariance matrix &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\boldsymbol{\beta}_{\text{grad}} = \nabla \ln \bar{W}" /> = Selection gradient vector on fitness <Katex tex="W" />.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 8 -->
    <section id="liability">
      <h2>8. Falconer's Liability Threshold Model</h2>
      <p>
        For complex binary disease traits (e.g. Type 2 Diabetes, Schizophrenia, Crohn's Disease), phenotypes are discrete (affected vs unaffected), but underlying genetic susceptibility is continuous and polygenic.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Falconer's Liability Threshold Model (1965)</strong> postulates an unobserved continuous latent variable called <strong>liability</strong> (<Katex tex="x \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)" />). An individual develops clinical disease if and only if their liability exceeds a critical threshold <Katex tex="T" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Liability Threshold & Population Disease Prevalence</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="K = P(\text{Affected}) = P(x \ge T) = 1 - \Phi(T) \implies T = \Phi^{-1}(1 - K)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="K" /> = Population prevalence &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\Phi" /> = Cumulative standard normal distribution function.
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Among affected individuals, the mean liability is elevated by truncation selection:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Mean Liability of Affected Individuals</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="i = \mathbb{E}[x \mid x \ge T] = \frac{\phi(T)}{K} = \frac{z_K}{K}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="z_K = \phi(T) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-T^2 / 2}" /> is the standard normal probability density at threshold <Katex tex="T" />.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

